"Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the closed neighborhood ideal of a graph (https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04683)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Title: Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the closed neighborhood ideal of a graph\nTitle: Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the closed neighborhood ideal of a graph\nAuthors: Shiny Chakraborty, Ajay P. Joseph, Amit Roy, Anurag Singh\nDate: Tue Jan  9 17:25:33 2024\n\nLet $G$ be a finite simple graph and let $NI(G)$ denote the closed neighborhood ideal of $G$ in a polynomial ring $R$. We show that if $G$ is a forest, then the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of $R/NI(G)$ is the same as the matching number of $G$, thus proving a conjecture of Sharifan and Moradi in the affirmative. We also show that the matching number of $G$ provides a lower bound for the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of $R/NI(G)$ for any $G$. Furthermore, we prove that, if $G$ contains a simplicial vertex, then $NI(G)$ admits a Betti splitting, and consequently, we show that the projective dimension of $R/NI(G)$ is also bounded below by the matching number of $G$, if $G$ is a forest or a unicyclic graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCastelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the closed neighborhood ideal of a graph - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2401.04683v4)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the closed neighborhood ideal of a graph ... We show that if $G$ is a forest, then the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of $R/NI(G)$ is the same as the matching number of $G$, thus proving a conjecture of Sharifan and Moradi in the affirmative.\n\n## Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the closed neighborhood ideal of a graph\n\nShiny Chakraborty, Ajay P. Joseph, Amit Roy, Anurag Singh\n\nPublished: 2024-01-09 | Updated: 2024-10-18\n\nComments: Final version. To appear in the Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics\n\nNote:\n\nJournal: Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics, 61(1), 2025\n\nDOI: 10.1007/s10801-024-01369-0\n\ncastelnuovo-mumford graph polynomial ring simple graph matching polynomial\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nLet $G$ be a finite simple graph and let $NI(G)$ denote the closed neighborhood ideal of $G$ in a polynomial ring $R$. We show that if $G$ is a forest, then the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of $R/NI(G)$ is the same as the matching number of $G$, thus proving a conjecture of Sharifan and Moradi in the affirmative. We also show that the matching number of $G$ provides a lower bound for the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of $R/NI(G)$ for any $G$. Furthermore, we prove that, if $G$ contains a simplicial vertex, then $NI(G)$ admits a Betti splitting, and consequently, we show that the projective dimension of $R/NI(G)$ is also bounded below by the matching number of $G$, if $G$ is a forest or a unicyclic graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInclusion-exclusion on Schubert polynomials (https://alco.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/alco.200.pdf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Inclusion-exclusion on Schubert polynomials ... Gao’s conjecture 1.4, Theorem 1.5 and its proof.\nInclusion-exclusion on Schubert polynomials\n\n3.3. Gao’s conjecture 1.4, Theorem 1.5 and its proof. Gao [10] defined a sequence of integers \\(\\{c_u\\}_{m\\ge 1,\\,u\\in S_m}\\) recursively, as follows:\n\n(14) \\[\nc_w := \\mathfrak{S}_w(1) - 1 - \\sum_{|u|<|w|} c_u p_u(w),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(|u|=m\\) if \\(u\\in S_m\\), and \\(p_u(w)\\) is the number of occurrences of \\(u\\) as a pattern in \\(w\\).\n\nGao showed that \\(c_w=0\\) whenever \\(w(n)=n\\), so the definition of \\(c_w\\) can be extended to all \\(w\\in S_\\infty\\). In the same paper, he conjectured the following:\n\nCONJECTURE 3.12 ([10, Conjecture 3.2]). We have \\(c_w \\ge 0\\) for all \\(w\\in S_\\infty\\).\n\nNotice that \\(p_u(w)=\\#\\{ \\text{words } v \\text{ such that } u=\\mathrm{perm}(v) \\text{ and } v\\le w\\}\\). Thus, we can rewrite (14) as\n\n(15) \\[\nc_w = \\mathfrak{S}_w(1) - \\sum_{v<w} c_v,\n\\]\n\nwhere the \\(-1\\) has been absorbed into the sum as \\(c_0\\). Note that this perspective explains the equivalence of Conjectures 1.4 and 3.12.\n\nBy inclusion-exclusion, (15) is equivalent to\n\n(16) \\[\nc_w = \\sum_{v\\le w} (-1)^{|w|-|v|}\\mathfrak{S}_v(1).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35550)\nciteturn6academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Gao, Matthew H. ... We prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. ... The identity follows from the covaluative formula of Ferroni and Schröter together with recurrences for its correction polynomials, derived from Ferroni's enumeration of admissible Delannoy paths for Schubert matroids. Since all three matroids on the right have fewer elements, the identity supplies the induction step in the proof of nonnegativity.\nTitle: Nonnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids\nAuthors: Alice L. L. Gao, Matthew H. Y. Xie\nDate: Mon Sep 28 16:25:02 2026\n\nWe prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. Our proof uses a deletion--contraction identity obtained by constructing an auxiliary split matroid. We first show that every simple, cosimple, connected split matroid $M$ has an element $e$ for which both the deletion $M\\setminus e$ and the contraction $M/e$ are connected. More generally, let $M$ be any connected split matroid of rank $k$ on a ground set $E$ with $|E|\\ge4$. Suppose that $e\\in E$ is such that both $M\\setminus e$ and $M/e$ are connected. For every $v\\in E\\setminus\\{e\\}$, we construct a connected elementary split matroid $N_{e,v}$ of rank $k-1$ on $E\\setminus\\{e,v\\}$ satisfying \\[ g_M(t)=g_{M\\setminus e}(t)+g_{M/e}(t)+t\\,g_{N_{e,v}}(t). \\] Using a fixed total order on $E$, we prescribe the proper cyclic flats of $N_{e,v}$ and their ranks. The identity follows from the covaluative formula of Ferroni and Schröter together with recurrences for its correction polynomials, derived from Ferroni's enumeration of admissible Delannoy paths for Schubert matroids. Since all three matroids on the right have fewer elements, the identity supplies the induction step in the proof of nonnegativity.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSquarefree powers of closed neighborhood ideals (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15229)\nciteturn6academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; In addition, we investigate the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the $ν$-th squarefree power of the closed neighborhood ideal of trees and show that this number can be arbitrarily larger than the degree of the ideal.\nTitle: Squarefree powers of closed neighborhood ideals\nAuthors: Marie Amalore Nambi, Ayesha Asloob Qureshi\nDate: Mon Mar 16 13:05:17 2026\n\nIn this article, we characterize all trees whose highest non-vanishing squarefree power of the closed neighborhood ideal is componentwise linear. In addition, we investigate the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of the $ν$-th squarefree power of the closed neighborhood ideal of trees and show that this number can be arbitrarily larger than the degree of the ideal. Finally, we give a formula for the regularity of $ν$-th squarefree power of the closed neighborhood ideal of caterpillar graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2609.35550v1)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Gao, Matthew H. ... We prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. ... The identity follows from the covaluative formula of Ferroni and Schröter together with recurrences for its correction polynomials, derived from Ferroni's enumeration of admissible Delannoy paths for Schubert matroids. Since all three matroids on the right have fewer elements, the identity supplies the induction step in the proof of nonnegativity.\n\n## Nonnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids\n\nAlice L. L. Gao, Matthew H. Y. Xie\n\nPublished: 2026-09-28 | Updated: 2026-09-29\n\nComments: 25 pages\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nmath.CO\n\nenumeration cyclic flat flat ground set matroid schubert polynomial\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. Our proof uses a deletion--contraction identity obtained by constructing an auxiliary split matroid. We first show that every simple, cosimple, connected split matroid $M$ has an element $e$ for which both the deletion $M\\setminus e$ and the contraction $M/e$ are connected. More generally, let $M$ be any connected split matroid of rank $k$ on a ground set $E$ with $|E|\\ge4$. Suppose that $e\\in E$ is such that both $M\\setminus e$ and $M/e$ are connected. For every $v\\in E\\setminus\\{e\\}$, we construct a connected elementary split matroid $N_{e,v}$ of rank $k-1$ on $E\\setminus\\{e,v\\}$ satisfying \\[ g_M(t)=g_{M\\setminus e}(t)+g_{M/e}(t)+t\\,g_{N_{e,v}}(t). \\] Using a fixed total order on $E$, we prescribe the proper cyclic flats of $N_{e,v}$ and their ranks. The identity follows from the covaluative formula of Ferroni and Schröter together with recurrences for its correction polynomials, derived from Ferroni's enumeration of admissible Delannoy paths for Schubert matroids. Since all three matroids on the right have fewer elements, the identity supplies the induction step in the proof of nonnegativity.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPattern bounds for principal specializations of beta-Grothendieck polynomials (https://alco.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/alco.422.pdf)\nciteturn6search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; We prove a conjecture of Yibo Gao in the setting of 1243-avoiding permutations that gives a lower bound for ν_w in terms of the permutation patterns contained in w. ... Schubert polynomials were introduced by Lascoux and Schützenberger [10] as canonical representatives for Schubert cycles in the cohomology ring of the complete flag variety 𝔽ℓ(n), which can be identified with a quotient of the polynomial ring ℤ[x_1, . . . , x_n] by the ideal generated by symmetric polynomials with no constant term.\nAlgebraic Combinatorics  \nVolume 8, issue 3 (2025), p. 745–763  \nhttps://doi.org/10.5802/alco.422\n\nPattern bounds for principal specializations  \nof β-Grothendieck polynomials\n\nHugh Dennin\n\nABSTRACT There has been recent interest in lower bounds for the principal specializations of Schubert polynomials ν_w := 𝒮_w(1, . . . , 1). We prove a conjecture of Yibo Gao in the setting of 1243-avoiding permutations that gives a lower bound for ν_w in terms of the permutation patterns contained in w. We extend this result to principal specializations of β-Grothendieck polynomials ν_w^(β) := 𝒢_w^(β)(1, . . . , 1) by restricting to the class of vexillary 1243-avoiding permutations. Our methods are bijective, offering a combinatorial interpretation of the coefficients c_w and c_w^(β) appearing in these conjectures